# config/Kconfig.domain — Application domain
#
# The top-level choice of what the image is for. AZOS is a general-purpose
# hybrid kernel; a robot is one worked example. The domain decides which
# crates are linked (DOMAIN_ROBOT -> kernel feature `domain-robot`, emitted by
# tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py), which menus `make config` shows, and, through
# `imply` and conditional defaults, the starting value of other options. It
# never forces one: every implied option can be changed by hand (wave 11).

menu "Application domain"

choice
    prompt "Application domain"
    default DOMAIN_GENERIC
    help
      What this image is for. Only Robot links the robot crates under
      domains/robot/ (behavior and the brain link, safety-core, flight,
      the ESC and RC receiver drivers) plus the IMU / barometer / GPS
      drivers and the robot shell commands. Every domain links the
      shared actuation authority (crates/core/actuation: e-stop latch,
      signed release, flight recorder, system watchdog). The other
      domains change defaults only, with `imply` or a conditional
      default, never `select`.

    config DOMAIN_GENERIC
        bool "Generic"
        help
          Default. A general-purpose kernel: scheduler, IPC, file
          systems, network, drivers, ring-3 services, and the shared
          actuation authority (e-stop latch, flight recorder, sys-wdt).
          No robot crate is linked and no robot task is started: built
          with `--no-default-features` plus the rest of the kernel's
          default features (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py). The in-kernel
          shell answers the robot commands with "needs the Robot domain".

    config DOMAIN_ROBOT
        bool "Robot"
        help
          Links the robot crates (kernel feature `domain-robot`):
          behavior and the brain link, the motor control loop and the
          per-robot-type safety envelope (safety-core), IMU / barometer /
          GPS / AHRS, flight, navigation, telemetry, the robot shell
          commands and the in-kernel benches, and starts their tasks.
          Shows the "Robot" menu: robot type, brain link, the
          safety-layer timers and the motor control loop.

    config DOMAIN_IOT_HMI
        bool "IoT / HMI (display, touch, camera, battery)"
        imply DRV_DISPLAY_HDMI
        imply DRV_DISPLAY_RAMFB
        imply DRV_CAMERA
        help
          Devices with a display, touch input, an optional camera and a
          battery. Implies (default on, can be turned off under
          "Drivers"): the display driver of the board (DRV_DISPLAY_HDMI
          on the VF2, DRV_DISPLAY_RAMFB on QEMU riscv64; neither exists
          elsewhere) and DRV_CAMERA (CSI camera brought up at boot,
          frames to ring 3 through SYS_SENSOR_READ_TYPED). The INA219 /
          ADS1115 drivers that read a supply voltage and current are in
          every image. There is no touch-controller driver and no
          battery state-of-charge model in the tree.

    config DOMAIN_GATEWAY
        bool "Gateway / edge / fleet"
        help
          A node that aggregates many downstream devices. On the Edge
          deployment profile it changes the network defaults (each can be
          set by hand under "Resource Limits" / "Network"):
            TCP_MAX_CONNS 8 -> 64, TCP_BUF_SIZE 131072 -> 16384:
              64 x 38,880 B = 2,488,320 B of TCP tables, against
              8 x 268,256 B = 2,146,048 B on Edge (+342,272 B);
            MAX_SOCKETS 16 -> 128 (+112 UDP-sized slots);
            ARP_CACHE_SIZE 16 -> 64, ARP_PENDING_SIZE 8 -> 16,
            DNS_CACHE_SIZE 4 -> 16, UDP_RX_SLOTS 4 -> 16.
          Smaller per-connection buffers for many more connections: a
          gateway holds many mostly idle links, not a few bulk ones.
          The Fleet profile (PROFILE_FLEET) keeps its own, larger tables.

    config DOMAIN_INDUSTRIAL
        bool "Industrial / automotive"
        help
          Control equipment with timing and fault-containment needs.
          Starts the threat model at "Physical access to the device"
          ("Mitigations"; can be changed there), with what that profile
          implies. Existing pieces: the CAN
          driver (crates/drivers/bus/src/can.rs), the control-loop
          rollback (CONTROL_TXN_TICKS) and lease priority inheritance
          (LEASE_PRIORITY_INHERITANCE) under "Scheduling, IPC & timing".
          The production scheduler is the Legacy priority backend; no
          deadline scheduler runs in a default image.

    config DOMAIN_MICROKERNEL
        bool "Microkernel (drivers as ring-3 servers)"
        imply DRV_RING3_DEFAULT
        help
          Drivers run as ring-3 server processes, as in a Mach/Hurd-style
          system. Implies DRV_RING3_DEFAULT (under "Drivers"), which
          starts every per-driver placement choice (DRV_<NAME>_PLACEMENT)
          at `ring3`; each can be changed by hand. Drivers a safety
          decision reads have no placement choice and stay in the kernel
          (the closed list is in config/Kconfig.drivers). Today the INA219
          and the buzzer have a placement choice (each defaults to ring3
          in every domain). The ring-3 drivers are started
          from their topology rows on BOARD_QEMU only
          (azos_topology feature ring3-driver-start, from the
          kernel's `qemu` feature).

endchoice

endmenu # Application domain
